		SCHEME-81 Symbol Tables


(Note: All numbers are in octal.)


;;;; Control PLA programming

Each state is introduced by its code number.  The next line contains
the name of the state and its entry point (if any).  Note that if
several states were merged, the state name is an arbitrary member of
the merged set.  The State-cref table can be used to locate the number
of any state.

The following line is the code number for the predicate selected by
that state.

The next (up to three) lines contain the next-state information
generated by the current state.  Each line contains the field,
followed by the state code number generated, followed by the name of
that state.

The next lines contain the name of various output fields and their
code values.  The following tables are used to decode these values:

Field		table

Predicate	Predicates
Op-code		Op-table
Non-uarg-op	Non-uarg-ops
Arg1-data	Uarg-ops
Arg2-data	Uarg-ops
Target-data	Uarg-ops
Explicit-load	Uarg-ops
Explicit-read	Uarg-ops
Constant	Constants


Also indicated here are the sources for the READ and LOAD busses,
indicated by "Read-source" and "Load-source".

Following this are any random assertions made by the state.  These
will take the form of field specifications of subfields of
RANDOM-SIGNALS, GC-FLAGS, and MEMORY-WAIT.


;;;; State-cref

Each line begins with the name of a state.  Following this is the code
number of the state.  Two pieces of information may then follow
depending on the state:
If the state was merged with other states and one of those other
states was actually used in the Control PLA progamming table, the state
that was actually used will be introduced by "Merged with:".
If the state (or anything it is merged with) was an entry point, this
is introduced by "Entry-point:".


;;;; Op-table

The ns-control PLA and the uarg-control PLA recieve the same input
from the control PLA.  The input value codes four pieces of
information: the next-state opertions if the selected predicate is
true; the next-state operations if the predicate is false; the
uarg-system operations if true; and the uarg operations if false.

The Op-table table indicates for each code value, the four operations
coded.  These operations are indicated as field specifications, each
of which corresponds to a control line of the appropriate data-path
object.  If an operation is to output several signals, they are placed
in a list whose CAR is "ALL-OPS".  Also, though each field
specification refers to one output of a PLA, it may be connected to
several control lines of a data-path object.


;;;; Predicates

Following the code is the predicate in DNF.  Each primitive predicate
is either a sense line from the main-regs (its last element is
"DATA-SENSES"), or from a pad (last element is "PAD-SENSES").


;;;; Uarg-ops

Each of these specifies either a (subfield of a) register or a
collection of them (in a list whose CAR is "ALL-OPS").  If one of
these values is placed on the LOAD bus, it is turned by the UARG-LOAD
PLA into a "load-global" signal to the register(s), if placed on the
READ bus, it is turned into a "read" signal by the UARG-READ PLA.


;;;; Non-uarg-ops

Each element is a (set of) operations for the main register file (last
element is "MAIN-REGISTERS") or for external registers (last element
is "EXTERNAL-REGISTERS". 


;;;; Constants

Each element contains the values to be inserted into the constant
sources of the main-registers.


;;;; Dispatch Program

Unlike the rest of the PLAs, the dispatch decoder isn't connected to
the control system.  It just translates type codes into one two kinds
of dispatch address.  This table gives the translations.
